---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

// Issue #1105: "Privacy and security." States the offline-first, no-ads,
// no-tracking stance, the age policy per PRIVACY.md §5, and — plainly —
// that sync is not end-to-end encrypted per PRIVACY.md §6. Links the
// canonical policy at /privacy (issue #1101). Every claim carries a
// `cite:` comment and a site/claims.md entry (checked by
// test/site/site_claims_test.dart).

const description =
  "How lunarlog protects cycle data: locked behind your device's own " +
  "security, no ads or tracking, and a plain statement of what cloud sync " +
  "is — and is not.";
---

<BaseLayout title="Privacy and security" description={description}>
  <article class="doc">
    <h1 class="doc__title">Privacy and security</h1>
    <p class="doc__lede">
      This app carries some of the most personal data a family has. Here is
      exactly how it is protected, what is shared when you turn sync on,
      and where the honest limits are. The full policy lives at
      <a href="/privacy">lunarlog.app/privacy</a>; the app's own Privacy
      screen summarizes it and links here.
    </p>

    <section>
      <h2>The stance</h2>
      <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §1 "No Advertising or Data Brokers"; §3 ("We do not use your information for targeted advertising, marketing communications, automated profiling, or behavioral tracking"). -->
      <ul>
        <li>No advertising, no data brokers, no behavioral tracking.</li>
        <li>No data sale, and no third parties beyond a short, named list in
          the policy.</li>
        <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §2.D (client-side scrubber, lib/observability/scrub.dart) and §1 "Minimal, Scrubbed Telemetry"; crash reporting is optional per §2.D. -->
        <li>Crash reports, when crash reporting is on, are scrubbed on the
          device first — health data, notes, dates, and identity are
          removed before anything is sent.</li>
        <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §2.B ("creating one remains your choice") and §1 "Resilient Offline". -->
        <li>An account is optional: logging, history, and estimates work
          without one, and before you sign in nothing leaves the device.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>On your device</h2>
      <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §6 (Device Encryption, Biometric Security, Optional In-App PIN, Inactivity Timeout, Screen Obfuscation, Backup Exclusion bullets). -->
      <p>
        The local database sits inside your device's own encryption (iOS
        Data Protection or Android device encryption), and its contents are
        shown only after your face, fingerprint, or passcode. On top of
        that: an optional in-app PIN, automatic relocking after inactivity
        plus immediate locking when you leave the app, and app-switcher
        previews that are masked. The database is also excluded from cloud
        backups on both platforms, so a copy of it never leaves your
        control.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>In sync — stated plainly</h2>
      <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §6 "Cloud Storage Encryption (not end-to-end)" — the honest posture #1105 requires. -->
      <p>
        <strong>Sync is not end-to-end encrypted.</strong> When you sign in
        and turn sync on, entries travel over TLS (1.2/1.3) and are stored
        in Supabase's Postgres, which encrypts data at rest with AES-256 —
        the same platform-level protection any well-run cloud database
        uses. lunarlog does not encrypt entry content on your device before
        it reaches Supabase: your entries are readable Postgres columns
        protected by row-level security and Supabase's own infrastructure
        controls, not by a cipher only your devices hold. That is the
        trade-off, stated in the open, and the policy's security section
        says the same.
      </p>
      <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §6 "Database Row-Level Security"; §1 "Protected at Rest". -->
      <p>
        What row-level security means in practice: the database refuses to
        return a row to anyone whose guardian memberships don't entitle
        them to it. Each accepted guardian — each role, exactly — sees what
        <a href="/family-sharing">their role</a> allows, and no more.
      </p>
      <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §6 "Browser Client (Web)" (nothing at rest; the access token in page memory; the refresh token in an HttpOnly cookie); webapp/e2e/storage-empty.spec.ts; webapp/worker/cookies.ts. -->
      <p>
        The browser app is built to a stricter rule than the phone apps:
        it keeps nothing at rest. No copy of your entries is ever written
        to browser storage, so closing the tab leaves none of them behind
        in that browser. What keeps you signed in is a cookie that no
        script on the page can read; the short-lived token the page itself
        uses lives only in the open tab's memory. The policy's security
        section describes exactly what that means.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What never leaves with your data</h2>
      <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §4 FCM row (push token + internal profile id only; never a note, tag, flow level, date, or profile name; fixed generic text). -->
      <p>
        Guardian alerts ride the same push infrastructure as any app's
        notifications, but the payload carries only a push token and a
        profile identifier — never a note, tag, flow level, date, or
        profile name. What appears on a lock screen is fixed generic text.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Family data, including minors'</h2>
      <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §5 "Minimum-Age Policy" (13+ alone; under-13 only through the parent's "her own profile" invitation, which is the consent record; issue #957); §5 "How Minor Status Is Determined"; §5 "Guardian Notes Are Visible To The Child" (issue #800's transparency). -->
      <p>
        lunarlog is licensed for people 13 and older. Someone under 13 does
        not sign up: a parent creates the profile and invites them through
        the app, and accepting that invitation is the record of parental
        consent. Minor profiles get exactly the same protections as adult
        ones — the same row-level security, the same transport and storage
        encryption — and nothing about a minor's profile is shared or
        analyzed. In keeping with the app's own transparency, guardian
        notes on a young person's profile are visible to every guardian —
        including the young person, once they hold an account on their own
        profile. The app never offers a hidden note about a child.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Deleting, and how long things are kept</h2>
      <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §7 "Account & Cloud Deletion" ("immediately and permanently... normally gone by the time the confirmation completes"); §7 "Server-Side Retention Windows" (30/90/180-day and 1-year hard deletions of bookkeeping records); walkthrough at /delete-account. -->
      <p>
        Deleting your account in the app deletes the server rows and the
        account immediately and permanently — not a request queued for
        later. Individual entries and whole profiles can be deleted any
        time. A few narrow bookkeeping records (an alert delivery record,
        merge disclosures, change history) delete themselves on fixed
        schedules, measured in weeks or months — the policy's retention
        section lists every one.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="doc__footer-note">
      The complete policy, versions and change history included:
      <a href="/privacy">lunarlog.app/privacy</a>. Questions:
      <a href="/support">Support</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
